---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToGif from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToGif.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="video-to-gif">
  <VideoToGif client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to turn a video into a GIF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop a video in. The tool selects the first six seconds, a good length for most GIFs.</li>
      <li>Drag the two handles to the exact moment you want, or play the video and press <strong>Set here</strong> to move a handle to the playhead.</li>
      <li>Choose a frame rate and width. 12 fps at 480 px is a good balance between smoothness and file size.</li>
      <li>Leave the two-pass palette on unless speed matters more; it improves quality more than any other setting.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create GIF</strong>, check the preview, and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why GIFs are large, and how to make them smaller</h2>
    <p>
      A GIF stores each frame as its own palettised image with only simple LZW compression. It has no motion
      prediction, so unlike H.264 it cannot say "this frame is the previous one shifted slightly". So the file
      size is roughly proportional to width × height × frames. These settings control it:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Lever</th>
            <th>Change</th>
            <th>Effect on size</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Length</td>
            <td>10 s → 5 s</td>
            <td>Halves it. The most effective and least visible change.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Width</td>
            <td>640 → 480 px</td>
            <td>Roughly −45%, because area scales with the square.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Frame rate</td>
            <td>24 → 12 fps</td>
            <td>Halves the frame count; looks fine for most screen captures.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dithering</td>
            <td>Sierra2 → none</td>
            <td>Around −20%, at the cost of visible colour banding.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the two-pass palette does</h2>
    <p>
      GIF allows at most 256 colours per frame. A simple converter maps your video onto a generic web palette,
      which turns skin tones grey-green and gradients into stripes. The two-pass method used here is better:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pass one, <code>palettegen</code>: FFmpeg scans each frame in your selected range and builds a 256-colour palette from the colours in the clip. It gives more weight to colours that change between frames (<code>stats_mode=diff</code>), so moving subjects keep their colours and the static background does not take over the palette.</li>
      <li>Pass two, <code>paletteuse</code>: each frame is mapped onto that custom palette with error-diffusion dithering, which hides the remaining quantisation as fine noise in place of hard bands.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      It takes about twice the encoding time and is worth it in almost every case. The exact filter chain is
      <code>fps=N,scale=W:-2:flags=lanczos</code> followed by the palette filters.
    </p>

    <h2>Dithering options compared</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Mode</th>
            <th>Look</th>
            <th>Use when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Sierra2 4A</td>
            <td>Fine, natural grain</td>
            <td>Default; photographic or filmed content</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Bayer</td>
            <td>Ordered cross-hatch pattern</td>
            <td>Flat UI colours; compresses better than error diffusion</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Floyd–Steinberg</td>
            <td>Smooth gradients, slightly noisier</td>
            <td>Sunsets, skies, soft lighting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>Hard colour edges</td>
            <td>Screenshots, logos, line art; smallest files</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>When not to use a GIF</h2>
    <p>
      For clips longer than about ten seconds, an MP4 or WebM is five to twenty times smaller at better
      quality, and chat apps, wikis and social networks now play short muted videos inline. Use GIF when the
      destination requires it, such as email signatures, some documentation systems and old forums. For other
      cases, use the <a href="/video-converter">video converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
